WEBVTT

00:00.666 --> 00:05.700
We present Video Pose Distillation for Few-Shot, Fine-Grained Action Recognition.

00:05.700 --> 00:09.266
This work is a collaboration between Adobe Research and Stanford University.

00:11.500 --> 00:15.100
Human centric sports represent a large class of interesting activities

00:15.100 --> 00:18.266
where large amounts of video data are available and where athletes

00:18.266 --> 00:22.133
perform a sophisticated set of acrobatic feats and technical actions.

00:23.800 --> 00:26.666
Performing tasks such as recognizing, detecting or retrieving

00:26.666 --> 00:29.500
these actions is an exciting area of research,

00:29.500 --> 00:32.233
and choosing the right feature representations is important.

00:32.233 --> 00:36.200
For video centered around athletes and their motion, pose-based methods

00:36.200 --> 00:41.666
leverage information about the human body, but they depend on accurate 2D pose estimation.

00:41.966 --> 00:46.800
This is problematic, because sports video is challenging for pose estimation.

00:46.800 --> 00:50.733
Motion is often most intense in the critical action

00:50.733 --> 00:53.033
frames, causing blur and ambiguity.

00:53.033 --> 00:57.433
Athletes also undertake extreme poses, seldom seen in daily life.

00:57.433 --> 01:01.366
Improving pose estimation is possible, but requires new pose

01:01.366 --> 01:04.600
annotations, which can be expensive to obtain.

01:07.433 --> 01:08.466
By contrast, end-to-end

01:08.466 --> 01:11.833
learning, for tasks such as action recognition, is often preferred.

01:11.833 --> 01:13.400
By training on the pixels

01:13.400 --> 01:16.566
directly, circumventing pose, these methods can be more reliable,

01:16.566 --> 01:20.433
even if pose estimates are not, but they require more labeled

01:20.433 --> 01:23.966
actions to train and are specialized to a particular task.

01:25.566 --> 01:28.166
This motivates the question:

01:28.166 --> 01:30.633
How do we learn reliable

01:30.633 --> 01:32.500
pose features for sports video that, like pose,

01:32.500 --> 01:35.033
benefit a variety of downstream tasks?

01:35.033 --> 01:37.466
And how do we do so without new pose annotations?

01:39.900 --> 01:44.400
We introduce Video Pose Distillation, or VPD, a novel method that combines

01:44.400 --> 01:49.400
the benefits of both pose and end-to-end methods.

01:49.400 --> 01:52.700
The inputs to VPD are an unlabeled corpus of video

01:52.700 --> 01:54.933
and a pose feature extraction pipeline, in this case

01:54.933 --> 01:58.400
consisting of a pretrained pose estimator and view-invariant pose embedding.

01:59.033 --> 02:01.800
Given each input video to VPD,

02:01.800 --> 02:04.300
there are two data pathways, the teacher and the student.

02:05.200 --> 02:08.033
The teacher takes the video and extracts

02:08.033 --> 02:11.100
pose features for the athlete using pretrained models.

02:11.100 --> 02:14.533
The student is a neural network that is distilled

02:14.533 --> 02:17.966
on the pixels, with the teacher’s outputs as weak-supervision.

02:17.966 --> 02:20.600
This setup is beneficial in several ways.

02:21.166 --> 02:24.466
First, note that no ground truth pose or action

02:24.466 --> 02:26.266
labels are required for distillation.

02:26.266 --> 02:28.266
VPD is agnostic to downstream tasks.

02:28.266 --> 02:31.366
Our key insight is that, by training the student

02:31.366 --> 02:35.400
over a large corpus of unlabeled video, the student learns features

02:35.400 --> 02:39.400
that agree with the teacher’s pose when the teacher is reliable.

02:40.633 --> 02:44.400
And, the student, with access to the pixels, is able

02:44.400 --> 02:48.166
to specialize to the visual patterns in the sport video domain

02:48.166 --> 02:52.333
and avoid the teacher’s mistakes when the teacher is unreliable.

02:52.333 --> 02:56.066
We also leverage temporal information, from video, to learn motion.

02:56.633 --> 03:00.533
This is done by encoding both the current frame and the previous

03:00.533 --> 03:04.600
frame with the teacher to obtain the current pose and its temporal derivative.

03:04.600 --> 03:08.333
The student receives the pixels and optical flow of the current frame

03:08.333 --> 03:10.600
and is tasked to produce a descriptor.

03:11.600 --> 03:14.300
A second network, to decode the student’s

03:14.300 --> 03:17.133
features, is used at training time to provide supervision for the student.

03:17.133 --> 03:21.700
This decoder has the added effect of allowing the student to produce features that differ from the teacher,

03:21.700 --> 03:25.000
encapsulating motion information as well as pose.

03:25.433 --> 03:30.533
At inference time, only the student is needed to produce features for downstream tasks.

03:31.066 --> 03:35.133
We find that VPD features outperform their teacher on fine-grained action

03:35.133 --> 03:37.800
recognition tasks on our four evaluation datasets

03:37.800 --> 03:41.266
when used as inputs to a generic GRU classifier.

03:43.700 --> 03:45.200
They also outperform or

03:45.200 --> 03:48.466
are competitive with state-of-the-art pose-based and end-to-end methods.

03:48.466 --> 03:53.433
In limited supervision settings, such as when only 8 to 64 examples

03:53.433 --> 03:57.100
per action class are available, VPD features perform well.

03:57.100 --> 04:01.633
Here are two datasets where pose estimation is the most challenging.

04:02.433 --> 04:06.400
VPD significantly outperforms its teacher and prior methods.

04:11.266 --> 04:14.666
Finally, VPD features are also effective for

04:14.666 --> 04:17.333
tasks such as action retrieval and detection.

04:19.766 --> 04:22.566
In summary, we have presented Video Pose Distillation,

04:22.566 --> 04:25.133
a novel method to learn robust pose

04:25.133 --> 04:28.266
features for challenging video datasets, without new pose labels,

04:28.266 --> 04:31.833
that combines the benefits of both pose and end-to-end learning.

04:31.833 --> 04:35.333
As a potential application of VPD, we hope that our work will improve

04:35.333 --> 04:36.666
analysis of interesting sports datasets

04:36.666 --> 04:39.700
and be beneficial for tasks such as labeling and curating new ones.

04:41.366 --> 04:43.733
Thank you for listening.

04:43.733 --> 04:46.400
Please check out our paper for additional experiments and details.

04:46.400 --> 04:49.466
Our code and data are also available at our website.
